var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Overview","page":"Overview","text":"RANSACScoringDoneRight is the scoring package of the paper RANSAC Scoring Done Right: the scale-free marginal score and its sort-and-sweep, Student-t posterior weighting, the threshold-based baselines it is compared against, and CPU/GPU grid evaluators that score a stored hypothesis pool at every value of a hyperparameter grid. It is problem-agnostic: a consumer supplies residuals, or a problem implementing VisualGeometryToolkitCore's AbstractConstraintProblem.\n\nIt sits in a stack of Julia packages:\n\npackage role\nVisualGeometryToolkitCore geometric types, Uncertain/whiten, the robust-loss shapes, the contracts (problem protocol, scoring, local optimization, pool selection), the Student-t posterior toolkit, the MSAC/RANSAC baseline scorers, and the benchmark pool record PoolPair\nRANSACScoringDoneRight scoring: the marginal score and the paper baselines on the scoring contract, their grid evaluators on the pool-selection contract; depends on VisualGeometryToolkitCore, and on VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation for SIMFIT++\nVisualGeometryToolkitEstimation the RANSAC loop, the concrete problems (homography, fundamental and essential matrix, …), the core local optimizations and SIMFIT++\nLocalOptimizationDoneRight the state-of-the-art local-optimization strategies\nVisualGeometryToolkitEval pool IO, statistics, tuning (including the scale-prior fits), the evaluation engine, rendering\n\nA caller composes them: VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's find_* take a scorer from this package and a strategy from LocalOptimizationDoneRight as scoring = …, local_optimization_strategy = ….","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Scoring-versus-selection","page":"Overview","text":"A RANSAC score assigns a value to one model hypothesis from its residuals; selection keeps the hypothesis with the best score. This package does both, at two granularities:\n\none hypothesis: score_residuals! (from residuals) or score_sq_residuals! (from a problem and a model) returns the score and the inlier count of the best partition;\na pool of hypotheses: VisualGeometryToolkitCore's best_pool_model selects the best stored model of one benchmark pair, and grid_evaluate does so for every pair of a staged pool at every width of a grid, which is what tuning and the paper's evaluation consume.\n\nThe scale-free marginal score also selects an inlier partition: the sweep returns the inlier count k it maximized over, and the scale of that partition (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's consensus_s2) follows in closed form. That partition and scale seed local optimization through the scoring contract (consensus_order, inlier_count, decision_boundary) and VisualGeometryToolkitCore's posterior-weighting layer (PosteriorWeightState).","title":"Scoring versus selection"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#The-contracts-this-package-implements","page":"Overview","text":"Both contracts are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's; this package implements them for its own types.\n\nScoring. StudentTUniformScoring implements scoring_workspace (a ScoringWorkspace), score!, inlier_count, consensus_order, decision_boundary, scale_prior, outlier_halfwidth, check_workspace and with_width/width_axis; its {Predictive} form adds is_predictive and scored_model. Any RANSAC written against the contract, VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's among them, runs it unchanged. The loss-width families at a loss width implement score! (with with_width/width_axis), what pool selection reads.\nPool selection. Every grid family here declares one staging (selection_staging, stage_selection: stage_ig_shared_from_pairs shared by all of them) and implements select_on_grid! with its device kernels. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's generic select_on_grid! and best_pool_model serve any scorer at its width.","title":"The contracts this package implements"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#What-the-weights-mean","page":"Overview","text":"A robust result carries per-point weights, and what they mean is a trait of the scorer's type, VisualGeometryToolkitCore's weight_kind; its inlier_mask cuts them by that kind.\n\nBinary. Every loss-width family here (MLESAC, MAGSAC++, the Welsch, smooth-TLS and Geman–McClure costs) is an AbstractThresholdScoring, as RANSAC and MSAC are, so its kind is BinaryWeights: a weight is the 0/1 consensus membership, and the inlier mask is w > 0. None of them has an outlier_halfwidth (it is nothing), so the weights stay that membership.\nPosterior. StudentTUniformScoring, at a fixed outlier half-width or with one of this package's outlier models (OutlierTerm, GeneralizedParetoOutliers, ExtremeValueHalfwidth), is PosteriorWeights: posterior inlier probabilities, cut by the MAP rule is_map_inlier (posterior > ½).\n\nusing RANSACScoringDoneRight\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore: weight_kind\n\nweight_kind(typeof(MagsacScoring(2)))                       # BinaryWeights()\nweight_kind(typeof(loss_width_scoring(\"welsch_cost\", 1)))   # BinaryWeights()\n\ntest/weight_kind_tests.jl holds these claims for every scorer the package defines.","title":"What the weights mean"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#The-scorers","page":"Overview","text":"The scale-free marginal score. For a fixed inlier partition the Gaussian inlier scale is integrated out under a conjugate inverse-gamma prior, leaving a closed form in the residual sum of squares, the inlier count and the outlier half-width a of the uniform outlier component. Sorting the residuals once lets one sweep score every partition, so a hypothesis costs O(N log N). StudentTUniformScoring is the spec (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's; its scale and label priors required keywords, the stack's defaults a configuration's: VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's [scoring.student_t_uniform]), ScoringWorkspace the sweep buffers. StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive} adds the model's estimation covariance to every point's residual covariance; it is an option, not the default (see Conventions).\n\nThe threshold-based family. RANSAC, MSAC, MLESAC (the paper's GaU) and MAGSAC++ are singleton types (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's RansacScoring and MsacScoring, MlesacScoring, MagsacScoring), together with three robust costs (Welsch, smooth TLS, Geman–McClure). They form the LossWidthScoring union: each takes a loss width, and each scores through one per-residual scalar, residual_quality. Every scoring carries its constraint codimension DG, because a loss width means a different multiple of the scale at each codimension.\n\nThe mixture's decision boundary. GauInlier is the Gaussian-uniform mixture; decision_boundary and its inverse decision_width are the one map between an outlier half-width and the residual at which the MAP inlier/outlier decision flips, so a grid of outlier half-widths is never confused with a grid of thresholds.","title":"The scorers"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Scale-priors","page":"Overview","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore's IGPrior(α₀, β₀) is the inverse-gamma prior on σ²; IGPrior() (also spelled JeffreysPrior()) is its α₀ = β₀ = 0 limit, the non-informative Jeffreys prior, which needs no validation data. In the conjugate update the prior acts as 2α₀ pseudo-residuals carrying 2β₀ of pseudo-RSS, so where a pair has many inliers the data dominate and the informative priors approach the Jeffreys limit.\n\nThe paper's empirical-Bayes variants fit the prior from per-pair noise-variance estimates on a validation set (VisualGeometryToolkitEval's Tuning.PriorSpec and Tuning.fit_prior):\n\nvariant prior fit\nJeffreys IGPrior(0, 0) none (JeffreysPriorSpec)\nIG-MLE IG(α₀, β₀) type-II maximum likelihood: a Gamma fit to 1/σ̂²\nIG-Robust IG(1, median(σ̂²)) fixed α₀ = 1, median-anchored β₀ (IGRobustSpec)\n\nIG-Robust trades the dispersion information of IG-MLE for robustness to the heavy σ̂² tails of pairs with few inliers, the regime where the prior matters.","title":"Scale priors"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Widths-and-native-units","page":"Overview","text":"Every scorer has one hyperparameter, a width, and each family's width lives on its own axis: the outlier half-width for the marginal scores, the loss width for the threshold-based family. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's width_axis(scoring) names a scorer's axis (OutlierHalfwidth or LossWidth); its to_native maps an efficiency constant (a width as a plain multiple of the noise scale) onto it and native_map is that map curried, so that grids for different families can be laid out over comparable neighbourhoods of the same noise scale. The vocabulary is fixed: efficiency_constant is that multiple, loss_width is a loss's scale parameter, outlier_halfwidth the half-width of the uniform outlier support, decision_boundary the residual at which the MAP rule flips, s2 a variance and ŝ/s_hat_sq the scale estimate and its square.","title":"Widths and native units"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#The-pool-frame","page":"Overview","text":"The benchmark pool — VisualGeometryToolkitCore's PoolPair record together with its num_points × num_models Float32 residual matrix — stores correspondences, models and residuals in NormalizedImageFrame: the image centre at the origin, scaled by half the image diagonal so the corners lie at unit radius. It is the one declared exception to the stack's rule that a public function returns results in the frame its inputs came in. Every width applied to pool data (outlier half-width, loss width, IG prior, every grid_evaluate grid) is in that frame's unit, and VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Pools.pixel_residuals is the one exit to pixels. Conventions states the rule and what it demands of this package.","title":"The pool frame"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Where-the-paper's-experiments-live","page":"Overview","text":"This repository holds the package and, in neurips2026/, the paper's research project: the manuscript, the drivers that draw its Profile and Sensitivity figures, their configs of record and the asset index neurips2026/REPRODUCE.md, plus the write-ups of the retired scoring studies (Experiments). The figures regenerate byte for byte from the six stored evaluation runs of record; the evaluation that produced those runs (pools, coarse probes, the per-pair grid evaluation and the bootstrap) predates the current stack and is documented in REPRODUCE.md but not yet ported. The bootstrap evaluation harness that once lived in this package moved to VisualGeometryToolkitEval.","title":"Where the paper's experiments live"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stack/#Working-on-the-stack","page":"Working on the stack","text":"This page is for people who change this package or the packages around it. It lists the VisualGeometryToolkitCore contracts the package implements, how the GPU grid evaluators and the SIMFIT++ grid are organised, the vocabulary the package adds to the stack's, and how the repository is laid out. The Overview explains the same pieces from a user's point of view.","title":"Working on the stack"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stack/#Contracts-implemented","page":"Working on the stack","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore owns the contracts. This package implements them for its own types and re-exports nothing VisualGeometryToolkitCore owns.\n\nThe scoring contract lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCore's robust/estimation.jl. StudentTUniformScoring implements scoring_workspace (its ScoringWorkspace), score! (the sort-and-sweep of Algorithm 1), inlier_count, consensus_order, scale_prior, outlier_halfwidth, check_workspace and with_width/width_axis (the axis is OutlierHalfwidth). Its decision_boundary is the MAP crossover of the consensus set's ŝ² against the uniform outlier box. StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive} also implements is_predictive and scored_model. VisualGeometryToolkitCore derives the consensus scale (consensus_s2), the soft inlier weights and the Student-t posterior toolkit from these methods.\n\nThe pool-selection contract lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCore's robust/pool_selection.jl. Every grid family of this package declares the :rsdr staging through selection_staging and stage_selection, and implements select_on_grid! over its device kernels. The families are the marginal, the width-free loss-width families, LossSpec, ScaleMarginal and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's SimFitPP. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's best_pool_model(scoring, pair, R) serves any scorer at its width; this package's loss-width families take it at a loss width, with_width(F(dg), LossWidth(w)), through their score!.\n\nProblems implement VisualGeometryToolkitCore's AbstractConstraintProblem: data_size, codimension, minimal_sample_size and constraint_with_jacobians. Predictive scoring also needs solver_covariance, declared by provides_solver_covariance. The RANSAC loop and the concrete problems are VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's. The local-optimization strategies are VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's and LocalOptimizationDoneRight's. A caller composes them, for example\n\nfind_essential(camera1, camera2, cs;\n               scoring = scoring_from_toml(Dict(\"type\" => \"student_t_uniform\", \"outlier_halfwidth\" => a);\n                                           problem = EssentialProblem),   # priors from the stack defaults\n               local_optimization_strategy = …)\n\nRANSAC and MSAC at a threshold (RansacScoring, MsacScoring) are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's baseline scorers. This package adds their grid methods.","title":"Contracts implemented"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stack/#GPU-scoring-with-KernelAbstractions","page":"Working on the stack","text":"The files ig_sweep_ka.jl, loss_width_sweep_ka.jl and marg_sweep_ka.jl hold device kernels that run on the CPU and on CUDA. They score a pool that is staged once with stage_ig_shared_from_pairs(pairs, residuals; backend) from the light pool records (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's PoolPair) and their residual matrices. One verb, grid_evaluate, evaluates a grid and dispatches on the scoring:\n\ngrid_evaluate([S,] loss_width_scoring(\"msac\", dg), staged, loss_widths; aggregation) evaluates the loss-width baselines. These are RANSAC, MSAC, GaU (MLESAC) and MAGSAC++, plus the Welsch, smooth-TLS and Geman–McClure losses. Each scores through one per-residual scalar, residual_quality(scoring, d², loss_width). The aggregation is LogBinned (bins_per_decade = 200, the fast cross-validation path). The exact selection (every residual) is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's select_on_grid! on its :pool staging (stage_selection(:pool, pairs, residuals)).\ngrid_evaluate(StudentTUniformScoring(problem_type, h; scale_prior, label_prior), staged, outlier_halfwidths) is the IG/Jeffreys marginal sweep. The grid replaces the scoring's own h.\ngrid_evaluate([S,] loss_spec(shape, prior; Δ, …), staged, outlier_halfwidths; model_dof, dg) is the Track-B redescending-loss mixture marginal.\ngrid_evaluate([S,] WelschMarginal(; prior_loss_width, outlier_halfwidth), staged, prior_loss_widths) evaluates the full-data scale marginals. The grid replaces the prior centre.\n\nVisualGeometryToolkitCore's width_axis(scoring) names the axis a scoring's grid is native on (OutlierHalfwidth or LossWidth), and this package states it for its grid families. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's to_native(axis, efficiency_constant, scale; d_g) maps an efficiency constant (a width as a plain multiple of the scale) onto that axis, and native_map(axis; d_g) is the same map curried.\n\nThe kernels run on CPU() or CUDA.CUDABackend(). The Float64 device path is bit-identical to the CPU reference; test/gate_marg_mix_ka.jl checks this and also runs on CUDA with --cuda. Each grid evaluator frees its function-local device buffers eagerly through VisualGeometryToolkitCore's free_device_buffers! instead of waiting for Julia's garbage collector.","title":"GPU scoring with KernelAbstractions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stack/#SIMFIT","page":"Working on the stack","text":"SIMFIT++ (Edstedt, CVPRW 2025, Parskatt/simfitpp) is VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's: its step-for-step port, SimFitPP, simfitpp_loss_width, simfitpp_select, unbiased_median_variance and with_residual_unit, moved there from this package on 2026-11-11 (SimFitPP). This package gives SimFitPP a grid on the pool-selection contract: for every initial loss width of the grid, simfitpp_select re-estimates the loss width on the pool and selects the MSAC-best hypothesis at the estimate.\n\nThe reference's bounds, 0.25 and 8, are pixel constants. A pool stores its data in NormalizedImageFrame (image centre, half diagonal), and no single scalar relates the two frames point by point. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Pools.pixel_residuals(pair) is the image-plane Sampson residual of the pool's models in pixels. It is computed from the light record and never reads the stored residual matrix, so it does not depend on that matrix's residual kind (tangent Sampson for E pools). The pair method of Estimation's simfitpp_select runs on it, so initial_loss_width_px, the bounds and the returned loss width are all in pixels. To work in the pool frame's unit instead, call the matrix method on the pair's residual matrix with bounds in that unit. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Pools.pool_pixel_scale(pair), the larger of the two views' pool-frame-per-pixel scales, carries a pixel quantity into the pool frame; SIMFIT++'s grid uses it for its initial guess only.","title":"SIMFIT++"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stack/#Scoring-and-tuning-vocabulary","page":"Working on the stack","text":"This package holds the vocabulary and the per-pair machinery. That is the scoring families' tag registry (ALL_SCORING_TAGS), the score! of its loss-width families at a loss width (which VisualGeometryToolkitCore's best_pool_model and VisualGeometryToolkitEval's sample-budget engine select with), precompute_pair and the KernelAbstractions grids above. A scoring at its width is its own pool-selection handle.\n\nOther layers live elsewhere. The prior-spec layer that fits IGPrior from validation data (PriorSpec, fit_prior) is VisualGeometryToolkitEval's Tuning. The pool record, PoolPair, and its accessors (num_points, num_models, pool_imc, pool_normalization and others) belong to VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Pools module. Pool IO belongs to VisualGeometryToolkitEval.\n\nThe layer is problem-agnostic. The concrete problem types (HomographyProblem, FundMatProblem, EssentialProblem) stay in the consumer. A scorer that needs one takes it as a leading problem_type argument, as in best_pool_model(StudentTUniformScoring(problem_type, outlier_halfwidth; scale_prior, label_prior), pair, R).","title":"Scoring and tuning vocabulary"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stack/#Names-of-quantities","page":"Working on the stack","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore's robust layer is authoritative for the loss words. The shape math is ρ, ψ and weight. An efficiency_constant is the dimensionless multiple of the scale at which a width sits, s2 is the noise variance, loss_width is a loss's scale parameter and sq_loss_width is its square (efficiency_constant² · s2 for a deployed loss). VisualGeometryToolkitCore writes the squared residual as s in ρ(shape, s, sq_loss_width). This package writes it sq_r or sq_distances, never s.\n\nOn top of that, this package names the following quantities.\n\nname quantity\noutlier_halfwidth the half-width of the uniform outlier component\ndecision_boundary the residual at which the MAP rule flips\ndecision_boundary / decision_width the one map between the two (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's generic, with the mixture's method), so a grid of outlier half-widths is never called a grid of thresholds\noperating_efficiency_constant the efficiency constant at which a protocol converts (loss_width = operating_efficiency_constant · ŝ)\nŝ, s_hat_sq the scale estimate and its square\nd_g / dg the constraint codimension\ninlier_prior / inlier_posterior the scalar mixing proportion π and the per-point P(inlier | r)\n\nA name that contains scale returns a linear scale (pool_pixel_scale). A name for a squared quantity says so (unbiased_median_variance, s_hat_sq, sq_loss_width). The one exception is fit_scale, which is named for the fit contract; its docstring states first that it returns the square.","title":"Names of quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stack/#Repository-layout","page":"Working on the stack","text":"src/                   the package: the sort-and-sweep marginal scorer, the grid\n                       evaluators (KernelAbstractions), the baseline families, SIMFIT++'s grid\ntest/                  the package's tests (ReTestItems)\ndocs/                  the documentation site (Documenter)\nneurips2026/           the research project (DrWatson layout, devs this package):\n  Project.toml         every dependency the drivers need (`@quickactivate \"NeurIPS2026\"`)\n  paper/               the manuscript (arXiv version); generated figures in paper/figures/\n  scripts/             the figure drivers (stage-idempotent), the data fetch and\n                       reproduce/ (the one-command reproduction per site)\n  configs/             configs of record (figures, prose-number check, pools, data catalog)\n  src/                 the shared prelude: the ported panel renderers, verify/deploy\n  test/                the project's tests (`julia --project=. test/runtests.jl`)\n  data/                exp_raw / exp_pro bindings (per machine, not tracked)\n  notes/               write-ups of the retired scoring studies\n  REPRODUCE.md         the figure → generator → config → data index, and what is not ported\n\nThe package, its tests, its docs and the research project are one Pkg workspace. They resolve together into one Manifest.toml at the repository root, which is not committed.\n\nThe split follows one rule. What a second paper or a user's RANSAC reuses (the scorers, their grids, the per-pair selection) belongs in the package. Drivers, figures, configs of record and stored results belong in neurips2026/. The package never depends on the project.","title":"Repository layout"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stack/#Development","page":"Working on the stack","text":"The stack's git hooks keep the local Manifest.toml resolvable: after a pull, checkout or rebase that changes the Project.toml of any workspace member, they re-resolve the workspace. They only resolve; precompilation waits for the next using. They are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's (its githooks/, one copy for every stack repository) and opt-in, once per clone:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using VisualGeometryToolkitCore; VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_githooks()'\n\nThe package's tests use ReTestItems, one item per test/*_tests.jl file. Pkg.test(; test_args = [\"select_on_grid\"]) runs the items whose name matches. The research project's suite is separate: cd neurips2026 && julia --project=. test/runtests.jl.","title":"Development"},{"category":"section","location":"api/posterior/#Posterior-weighting","page":"Posterior weighting","text":"After marginalizing the scale, the inlier density is a Student-t; the selected partition's scale estimate and the per-point posterior inlier probabilities follow in closed form. That toolkit — the scale estimators (IGPrior, fit_scale, partition_student_t), the Student-t + uniform mixture (StudentTMixture, posterior_weights!, posterior_inlier_probs!), the posterior-weight state and its expectation–conditional-maximization (ECM) updates (PosteriorWeightState, update_scale!, update_nu!, update_inlier_prior!, mixture_ecm!) and their update policies (Estimated, FixedS2, FixedPrior, FixedNu, ConsensusDof) — is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's, documented there.\n\nA scored partition seeds it through the scoring contract: after score! on a StudentTUniformScoring, the first inlier_count entries of consensus_order are the swept partition, and PosteriorWeightState(sq_distances, n, model_dof, k; order, prior = scale_prior(scoring), …) is its Student-t with the E-step run once.","title":"Posterior weighting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Pools,-grids-and-tuning","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"Scoring over a benchmark pool: VisualGeometryToolkitCore's light PoolPair record together with its num_points × num_models Float32 residual matrix, both in the pool frame NormalizedImageFrame (see Conventions). No function here opens a file; pool IO belongs to VisualGeometryToolkitEval.","title":"Pools, grids and tuning"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Per-pair-model-selection","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"One pair's selection is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's best_pool_model(scoring, pair, R) for a scorer at its width: the Student-t marginal, or a loss-width family at a loss width, with_width(F(dg), LossWidth(w)). The loss-width families are VisualGeometryToolkitCore AbstractThresholdScorings, as RANSAC and MSAC are, and score through Core's one threshold score!: Σ quality in index order, the minimal sample's points at zero residual, a consensus set d² < w², and under a running best an early exit that never changes the selection.","title":"Per-pair model selection"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Grid-selection:-the-pool-selection-contract","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore's select_on_grid!(idxs, P, scoring, grid, staged) fills idxs[p, t] with the model selected for pair p at width grid[t]. Every grid family of this package (StudentTUniformScoring, the width-free loss-width families, LossSpec, ScaleMarginal, SimFitPP) declares the staging :rsdr: stage_selection(selection_staging(scoring), pairs, residuals; backend) is one stage_ig_shared_from_pairs shared by all of them, and its select_on_grid! is one grid_evaluate (SIMFIT++: its host loop over simfitpp_select). pair_chunk_budget sizes the device chunks.","title":"Grid selection: the pool-selection contract"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Grid-evaluation-(CPU-and-GPU)","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"A pool is staged once (stage_ig_shared_from_pairs) and every scorer is evaluated over a grid of its native width by one verb, grid_evaluate, which dispatches on the scoring. The kernels are written with KernelAbstractions and run on CPU() or a CUDA backend.","title":"Grid evaluation (CPU and GPU)"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Native-widths","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"Each scorer's hyperparameter lives on its own width axis: the outlier half-width for the marginal scores, the loss width for the threshold-based family. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's width_axis(scoring) names it (this package states it for its grid families); the maps from an efficiency constant to the native width, to_native and native_map, and the conversions outlier_halfwidth_to_loss_width / loss_width_to_outlier_halfwidth (at an operating_efficiency_constant), are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's.","title":"Native widths"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Scale-marginals-of-redescending-losses","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"","title":"Scale marginals of redescending losses"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Scale-priors","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"The empirical-Bayes variants of the paper fit VisualGeometryToolkitCore's IGPrior from per-pair noise-variance estimates on a validation set: IG-MLE by type-II maximum likelihood, IG-Robust with α₀ = 1 and a median-anchored β₀. The fits (PriorSpec, fit_prior, ALL_PRIOR_SPECS) are VisualGeometryToolkitEval's Tuning; a fitted prior enters a scorer as StudentTUniformScoring(P, a; scale_prior, label_prior).","title":"Scale priors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#SIMFIT-loss-width-estimation","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"SIMFIT++ (Edstedt, CVPRW 2025; Parskatt/simfitpp) is VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's: SimFitPP, simfitpp_loss_width and simfitpp_select (exported there), unbiased_median_variance and with_residual_unit (public there). This package gives SimFitPP its width axis (LossWidth), its tag \"sim_fit_pp\" and its grid on the pool-selection contract (above).","title":"SIMFIT++ loss-width estimation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.precompute_pair","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"precompute_pair(pair::PoolPair, R) -> PrecomputedPair\n\nPer-pair staging precompute of pair and its residual matrix R (num_points × num_models, Float32, NormalizedImageFrame units). Each model column is squared in Float32, its minimal-sample points zeroed (exact inliers; an out-of-range index THROWS, as in every other scorer) and sorted; the selection error pool_imc is kept per model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.precompute_pair"},{"category":"function","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.pair_chunk_budget","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"pair_chunk_budget(M_max, T) -> Int\n\nThe number of pairs per chunk of a device selection grid.\n\nThe grid has T columns over pools of up to M_max models; the chunk bounds the score block (Float64, pairs × M_max × T) plus argmax's broadcast temporary under ~6 GiB, clamped to [1, 256]. T-aware, so a wide miscalibration grid (T up to ~600) stays in device memory and narrow validation grids keep the chunk large.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.pair_chunk_budget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.IGSharedData","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"IGSharedData{Backend, F32A, F64A, IntA}\n\nBackend-agnostic flat-layout view of every PrecomputedPair in a pool. Built once per pool by stage_ig_shared_from_pairs and reused by every grid evaluator.\n\nMemory layout — all distinct pairs flattened:\n\nsorted_sq_flat::F32A (a vector of the pool's residual eltype on backend)  contiguous concatenation of all (Ki × Mi) sortedsq matrices in  column-major order: pair 1 model 1 (length K1), pair 1 model 2, …,  pair 1 model M_1, pair 2 model 1, …\npair_offset::IntA (length P+1)  pair_offset[p] = absolute starting index of pair p in sorted_sq_flat.  pair_offset[P+1] = total length (sentinel).\nK_per_pair::IntA (length P)  — # residuals for each pair\nM_per_pair::IntA (length P)  — # models for each pair\nM_max::Int                   — max # of models across all pairs (kernel grid sizing)\nerrors_imc_flat::F64A          — concatenated per-model selection imc = pool_imc (H: Δrot; E/F: max(Δrot,Δtrans)), indexed by errors_offset[p] + j\nerrors_offset::IntA          — errors_offset[p] = start of pair p's imc segment\n\nP is the number of pairs in the pool.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.IGSharedData"},{"category":"function","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.stage_ig_shared_from_pairs","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"stage_ig_shared_from_pairs(pairs, residuals; backend) -> IGSharedData\n\nStage a pool once for every grid evaluator. The inputs are the light records pairs (AbstractVector{<:PoolPair}) with their residual matrices residuals (residuals[p] is pair p's num_points × num_models Float32 matrix, in NormalizedImageFrame units — the caller loads them; staging opens no file), each pair precomputed by precompute_pair into the flat IGSharedData layout on backend (by default VisualGeometryToolkitCore's default_device(): the accelerator when one is loaded and functional, else the CPU). No prior fit, no width grid baked in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.stage_ig_shared_from_pairs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.grid_evaluate","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"grid_evaluate(scoring::StudentTUniformScoring, staged::IGSharedData, outlier_halfwidths;\n              pair_chunk_size = 256, return_idxs = false) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe Student-t marginal grid (the IG/Jeffreys sort-and-sweep) on the staged pool's backend. It returns a (P, T) matrix of each pair's error at the model scoring selects at each outlier half-width in outlier_halfwidths (NormalizedImageFrame units). The grid REPLACES the scoring's own outlier half-width; its minimal sample size, codimension and scale prior are the scoring's. With return_idxs, also the (P, T) Int32 selected model indices.\n\nRuns ig_sweep_kernel! on the shared chunked driver (_chunked_argmax_eval): pair-chunks of pair_chunk_size bound the score block, and the model argmax is the shared argmax_models_kernel!, which takes the FIRST strictly best model and SKIPS a NaN score. The device sweep scores the model-certain marginal with the flat label prior and a supplied outlier half-width only: a StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive}, a non-flat label prior or an outlier model that estimates the support is an ArgumentError, never scored as something else.\n\n\n\n\n\ngrid_evaluate([S = Float32,] scoring::LossWidthScoring, staged::IGSharedData, loss_widths;\n              aggregation = LogBinned(), return_idxs = false, pair_chunk_size = 256)\n    -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nPer-pair selection errors of a loss-width family over a loss-width grid. They form a (P, T) matrix of the error (imc) of the model scoring selects at each loss width in loss_widths (NormalizedImageFrame units; with return_idxs, also the selected pool indices). Runs on the staged data's backend — KernelAbstractions CPU() or a GPU.\n\naggregation is the LogBinned aggregation (default 200 bins per decade; the fast cross-validation path) of the family's one per-residual quality. The exact selection (every residual) is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's select_on_grid! on the :pool staging. S is the bin-index precision (Float32, the default, is the production setting); scores accumulate in Float64.\n\nscoring is a LossWidthScoring member — build it with loss_width_scoring, supplying the problem's codimension.\n\n\n\n\n\ngrid_evaluate([S = Float32,] m::ScaleMarginal, staged::IGSharedData, prior_loss_widths;\n              NG = _MARG_NG, return_idxs = false, mem_budget = 6_000_000_000)\n    -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nFull-data scale-marginal scoring (WelschMarginal, SmoothTLSMarginal) at precision S (Float32 by default; Float64 for full precision), on the staged data's backend — KernelAbstractions CPU() or a GPU. The scale is integrated out by the package's one scale quadrature, the trapezoid logsumexp over NG log-scale nodes spanning t0 ± 7.0 around each grid point's prior centre (loss_spec(::ScaleMarginal)). Returns a (P, T) per-pair error matrix at the model each grid-point's marginal selects.\n\nprior_loss_widths is THIS FAMILY'S tuned parameter — the IG-prior centre on the loss width (prior_loss_width, NormalizedImageFrame units), not a decision threshold. Grid point t is m's family with m's prior shape alpha0, Z̃ domain m.outlier_halfwidth (the declared outlier support, POOL_FRAME_R_MAX on pool data), centred at prior_loss_widths[t]: the grid REPLACES m's own centre. return_idxs=true also returns the selected pool indices. The pair-chunk is sized so the NG-per-work-item scratch and the score block stay under mem_budget bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\ngrid_evaluate([S = Float32,] spec::LossSpec, staged::IGSharedData, outlier_halfwidths;\n              model_dof, dg, return_idxs = false, mem_budget = 6_000_000_000)\n    -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nTrack-B mixture-marginal grid evaluation, on the staged data's backend (KernelAbstractions CPU() or a GPU), the ONLY evaluation of this evidence. For the depth-Δ quadrature selector spec (built by loss_spec — Z̃ over the FIXED [0, r_max] domain), score every staged (pair, model) at every outlier half-width in outlier_halfwidths (NormalizedImageFrame units) and return the (P, T) per-pair error matrix at each grid-point's selected model (return_idxs=true also returns the selected pool indices).  Three kernels per pair-chunk: marg_mix_evidence_kernel! (E over k; the NG-node trapezoid quadrature), marg_mix_profile_kernel! (the outlier-half-width profile re-argmax over k), and the shared argmax_models_kernel!.  Precision S drives the evidence arithmetic — Float64 for full precision; Float32 is the fast production setting.  The pair-chunk is sized so the E block + the NG-per-work-item Dscratch stay under mem_budget bytes. model_dof (the minimal sample size, whose points are the exact inliers at the front of each column) and dg (the codimension) are the problem's, and REQUIRED: a LossSpec carries neither.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.grid_evaluate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.LogBinned","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"LogBinned(bins_per_decade = 200)\n\nThe fast cross-validation aggregation: residuals histogrammed on a log-spaced d² grid. The grid has bins_per_decade bins per decade, spanning 0.01·minlosswidth² to 100·maxlosswidth², and the family's quality is evaluated at the bin centres. Out-of-range and non-finite residuals score as outliers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.LogBinned"},{"category":"type","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.WelschMarginal","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"WelschMarginal(; alpha0=1.0, prior_loss_width, outlier_halfwidth)\n\nMarginal-Welsch scoring spec.  prior_loss_width is the IG-prior centre on the Welsch loss width (beta0 = alpha0·prior_loss_width² ⇒ 2·alpha0 pseudo-observations at pseudo-squared-width prior_loss_width²); outlier_halfwidth bounds the density normalizer Z̃ — REQUIRED, as for every scale-marginal spec. alpha0/beta0 are the same two IG hyperparameters IGPrior names. For an (alpha0, beta0) prior not tied to a centre, ScaleMarginal{WelschShape}(alpha0, beta0; outlier_halfwidth).\n\nThe evidence is the trapezoid scale quadrature of loss_spec(::ScaleMarginal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.WelschMarginal"},{"category":"type","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.LossSpec","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"LossSpec{K<:AbstractLossShape}\n\nThe depth-Δ quadrature-marginal spec of the redescending loss shape K: the shape, its well depth Δ, the quadrature grid with Z̃ baked at that depth over [0, r_max], and r_max.\n\nBuild it with loss_spec, which keeps Δ and the grid in step, and evaluate a staged pool with it through grid_evaluate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.LossSpec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.loss_spec","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"loss_spec(shape, prior; Δ, r_max=POOL_FRAME_R_MAX, t_lo, t_hi, NG=1537) -> LossSpec\n\nBuild a depth-Δ quadrature-marginal spec for the loss shape under IG scale prior. Z̃ is normalised over the FIXED domain [0, r_max] (the declared outlier support POOL_FRAME_R_MAX, in the pool frame's half-diagonal unit), so the outlier half-width is decoupled from the quadrature.  [t_lo, t_hi] are the log-scale grid bounds (use loss_scale_bounds(sq_distances, n)).\n\n\n\n\n\nloss_spec(m::ScaleMarginal{K}; NG=_MARG_NG) -> LossSpec\n\nThe quadrature of the full-data scale marginal m: shape K at its canonical depth, m's IG prior, Z̃ over [0, m.outlier_halfwidth] and NG log-scale nodes spanning m.t0 ± _MARG_L. The nodes lie on the ABSOLUTE lattice t = i·h, h = 2·_MARG_L/(NG − 1) (the domain snapped by at most h/2), so the specs of every grid point share their nodes and grid_evaluate(::ScaleMarginal, …) sums each model's data term once for the whole grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.loss_spec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/pools/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.loss_scale_bounds","page":"Pools, grids and tuning","text":"loss_scale_bounds(sq_distances, n; pad=4.0) -> (t_lo, t_hi)\n\nData-driven grid bounds on t = log sq_loss_width for a redescending-loss sweep over squared residuals sq_distances[1:n].  The scale-posterior peak for partition k lands near sq_loss_width ≈ RSS_k/(k−m), which ranges from ≈ the tightest residual scale (small k) to ≈ the all-point scale (k=n); the returned [t_lo, t_hi] brackets that range with pad decades (natural-log) of margin on each side so the trapezoid grid covers every k's peak.  Supply to loss_spec(...; t_lo, t_hi).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.loss_scale_bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"api/internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"Declared public because downstream packages (the paper scripts, the evaluation packages) reach into them, but low-level. They are not a stable interface.\n\nThe sweep, its workspace builders and accessors moved to VisualGeometryToolkitCore with StudentTUniformScoring (2026-11-11).","title":"Internals"},{"category":"section","location":"api/internals/#Not-public","page":"Internals","text":"Documented internals that the public docstrings refer to. They are not part of the API.","title":"Not public"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.PrecomputedPair","page":"Internals","text":"PrecomputedPair\n\nSorted squared residuals per model of one pool pair — the staging unit of stage_ig_shared_from_pairs: sorting once here makes every device sweep an O(n) linear scan over prefix sums.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.PrecomputedPair"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.ScaleMarginal","page":"Internals","text":"ScaleMarginal{K}(alpha0, beta0; outlier_halfwidth, t0 = nothing)\n\nThe scale-marginal spec of loss shape K at the IG prior (alpha0, beta0) on the squared loss width — e.g. one fit by type-II MLE (empirical Bayes), where beta0 is not tied to a prior centre. outlier_halfwidth bounds the density normalizer Z̃ (REQUIRED: it is the declared outlier support, POOL_FRAME_R_MAX in the pool frame's unit); t0 the centre of the log-scale domain, by default the prior mode log(beta0/(alpha0 + 1)) (or log((outlier_halfwidth/4)²) for an improper prior). THE one constructor of every family's spec: the keyword forms below only name the prior centre.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.ScaleMarginal"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.SmoothTLSMarginal","page":"Internals","text":"SmoothTLSMarginal(; alpha0=1.0, prior_loss_width, outlier_halfwidth)\n\nMarginal smooth-TLS scoring spec.  prior_loss_width is the IG-prior centre on the smooth-TLS loss width (beta0 = alpha0·prior_loss_width² ⇒ 2·alpha0 pseudo-observations at pseudo-squared-width prior_loss_width²); outlier_halfwidth bounds the density normalizer Z̃ (set to POOL_FRAME_R_MAX, the declared outlier support in the pool frame's unit — required, no default). alpha0/beta0 match IGPrior, as for WelschMarginal.  The evidence is the trapezoid scale quadrature of loss_spec(::ScaleMarginal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.SmoothTLSMarginal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#RANSACScoringDoneRight._build_score_weight_matrix","page":"Internals","text":"_build_score_weight_matrix(scoring, loss_widths, N_bins, d2_min, d2_max)\n    -> Matrix{Float32}  size (N_bins, T)\n\nPrecompute W[bin, t] = residual_quality(scoring, d²_bin, loss_width_t) on the host at the bin centres of a log-spaced grid. The centres are d²_bin = d²_min · (d²_max/d²_min)^((bin-0.5)/N_bins), and W holds the SAME scalar quality Core's exact kernel evaluates per residual. Returned column-major so the kernel can index W[bin + (t-1)·N_bins].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight._build_score_weight_matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Conventions","page":"Conventions","text":"The stack's shared conventions — naming, signatures, data arguments, frames and normalization, pixel origins, derivatives — are one canonical page in VisualGeometryToolkitCore's documentation, Conventions. This page does not restate them. It states the three things that are true of RANSACScoringDoneRight and of nowhere else in the stack.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#The-pool-frame-is-the-one-declared-exception-to-\"frame-in-frame-out\"","page":"Conventions","text":"Everywhere else the rule is pixels in, pixels out, with normalization private to one estimator. The benchmark pool is the exception, and it is declared rather than silent. The pool record — VisualGeometryToolkitCore's PoolPair (its Pools module documents the record, its maps and its accessors) — stores its correspondences and models in NormalizedImageFrame: image centre, scaled by HALF the image diagonal, so the image corners lie at unit radius. The record's residual matrix is not part of it: a pool's residuals are a separate num_points × num_models Float32 matrix in the same unit, and every pool scorer here takes the record AND its matrix — VisualGeometryToolkitCore's best_pool_model(scoring, pair, R), precompute_pair(pair, R), stage_ig_shared_from_pairs(pairs, residuals). No scorer opens a file.\n\nThe exception exists because the pool is shared STORAGE, not an estimator's input: a pool is written once and scored many times, across scenes whose images differ in size. Storing one frame keeps a single global outlier half-width and one IG scale prior correctly specified across the whole benchmark; converting per pair at scoring time would not.\n\nWhat the rule then demands of this package:\n\nEvery width on pool data is in the pool frame's unit and says so: the outlier half-width, the loss width, the IG scale prior, every grid_evaluate grid and POOL_FRAME_R_MAX.\nThere is one exit to pixels. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Pools.pixel_residuals is the image-plane Sampson residual of the pool's models in pixels, computed from the light record alone: the correspondences come back through the stored maps (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's pixel_correspondences), each model by composition with the normalization's sides (inv(D) ∘ M ∘ S). It never reads the stored residual matrix, so it does not depend on that matrix's residual kind — tangent Sampson for an essential pool, image-plane Sampson for F and H. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Pools.pool_pixel_scale (the larger of the two views' pixel → pool-frame scales) is the one scalar crossing; the two images are scaled independently, so no scalar relates the frames per point. SIMFIT++ uses it for a soft initial guess only, never for anything a bound gates.\n\nsimfitpp_select on a pool record is the deliberate exception to the exception: it is pixels in, pixels out, running on pixel_residuals so that SIMFIT++'s published pixel bounds (0.25–8 px) mean what they say.","title":"The pool frame is the one declared exception to \"frame in = frame out\""},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#POOL_FRAME_R_MAX-1-is-a-declared-constant,-not-a-tuning-knob","page":"Conventions","text":"POOL_FRAME_R_MAX is the upper limit of the domain [0, r_max] over which the density normalizer Z̃ of every redescending marginal (Welsch, smooth-TLS, Geman-McClure) is integrated. It is in the pool frame's half-diagonal unit.\n\nIt reads like a geometric bound, and it was first justified as one — under the FULL-diagonal frame every point lies within radius ½ of the centre, so no residual exceeds 1. Pools are written in the HALF-diagonal frame, where a point-to-point residual reaches 2, so that justification no longer holds and the constant truncates the outlier support at half the geometric maximum.\n\nIt stays 1 deliberately. Every constant tuned for these marginals — the cross-validated prior centres and outlier half-widths, and the published tables built on them — was cross-validated with r_max = 1, so the value is part of their definition, and one value keeps the truncation identical across scenes, probe and evaluation. Changing it moves every marginal score and invalidates every constant tuned against it.","title":"POOL_FRAME_R_MAX = 1 is a declared constant, not a tuning knob"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive}-is-an-option,-not-a-default","page":"Conventions","text":"StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive} incorporates the model's estimation covariance Σ̃_θ into the per-point scores. It is kept, and it is not the default, because it was measured rather than assumed.\n\nOn the reference rerun — 320 tasks, thresholds tuned per scoring AND per stage, paired seeds — against model-certain scoring, pooled ΔAUC@10°:\n\nProblem At selection After local optimization\nHomography −0.003 (tie) —\nAbsolute pose −0.001 (tie) —\nFundamental +0.011 −0.029\nEssential −0.010 +0.007\n\nIts one clear win is the essential matrix after local optimization at N = 60 (+0.016). It costs 13–82× per hypothesis at N = 60 and 68–565× at N = 250, even with the kinetic enumeration. The loss is largest where the predicted variance is most heterogeneous across inliers.\n\nTwo things that verdict does NOT say. The search is EXACT — the enumeration visits every top-k set attainable at any μ > 0 — so this is a property of the FIRST-ORDER predictive model, not of the implementation. And whether linearization is the cause is open: an oracle-covariance experiment settles it. The option stays for that reason.","title":"StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive} is an option, not a default"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#See-also","page":"Conventions","text":"Conventions — the shared text this page links to rather than restates\nAPI Reference — the functions named above","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/scoring/#Scoring","page":"Scoring","text":"The scale-free marginal score and its sort-and-sweep, on VisualGeometryToolkitCore's scoring contract, and the loss-width (\"threshold-based\") scorings it is compared against.","title":"Scoring"},{"category":"section","location":"api/scoring/#Problems-and-the-scoring-contract","page":"Scoring","text":"A problem is scored through VisualGeometryToolkitCore's constraint-problem protocol: it subtypes AbstractConstraintProblem and implements data_size, codimension, minimal_sample_size and constraint_with_jacobians; predictive scoring additionally needs solver_covariance, declared by provides_solver_covariance. A scorer implements VisualGeometryToolkitCore's scoring contract (scoring_workspace, score!, inlier_count, consensus_order, …), and the scale prior IGPrior (Jeffreys: JeffreysPrior()), the Student-t + uniform mixture toolkit and the Gaussian-uniform boundary (GauInlier, decision_boundary) are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's too. The concrete problems (homography, fundamental and essential matrix, …) live in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.","title":"Problems and the scoring contract"},{"category":"section","location":"api/scoring/#The-scale-free-marginal-score","page":"Scoring","text":"The Student-t + uniform marginal score itself is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's (moved there on 2026-11-11): StudentTUniformScoring carries the configuration (outlier model, model degrees of freedom, the scale and label priors, both required: the stack's defaults are VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's [scoring.student_t_uniform], read by scoring_from_toml), ScoringWorkspace the scratch buffers of the sort-and-sweep, and StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive} scores a model with its solver covariance. This package extends it by dispatch: its grids (grid_evaluate, select_on_grid!) and the outlier models below.","title":"The scale-free marginal score"},{"category":"section","location":"api/scoring/#Outlier-models-that-estimate-the-support","page":"Scoring","text":"Core's estimated-support sweep scores these through their methods of its hooks (outlier_cost, scored_outlier_halfwidth, outlier_context!); model-certain, codimension 1.","title":"Outlier models that estimate the support"},{"category":"section","location":"api/scoring/#Loss-width-scorings","page":"Scoring","text":"The baselines of the paper (RANSAC, MSAC, GaU/MLESAC, MAGSAC++) and three robust costs, each a type carrying its codimension and scored through one per-residual scalar, VisualGeometryToolkitCore's residual_quality. Each has VisualGeometryToolkitCore's two forms of RansacScoring/MsacScoring: width-free (F(dg), the grid family) and at a loss width (F(dg; threshold = w), or with_width(F(dg), LossWidth(w))), which selects by score!. RANSAC and MSAC are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's baseline scorers; this package adds their grid methods.","title":"Loss-width scorings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.TailRules","page":"Scoring","text":"TailRules(; pot_threshold_quantile, min_excess, finite_shape_limit)   # defaults: configs/default.toml [tail_rules]\n\nThe rules of the generalized Pareto tail fit.\n\nThe peaks-over-threshold (POT) rules both extreme-value outlier models share (Coles 2001, §4.3; Hosking & Wallis 1987): the generalized Pareto law is fitted by probability-weighted moments to the excesses over the POT threshold u, and pot_threshold_quantile is the quantile level of the complement's residuals at which the POT threshold u is set. Fewer than min_excess excesses, or a shape at or above finite_shape_limit (no finite endpoint), fall back to the largest residual of the complement; an endpoint below it is raised to it. The defaults (0.9, 5, -1e-3) are configs/default.toml's [tail_rules]. The retired keyword threshold_quantile is an ArgumentError naming pot_threshold_quantile.\n\nReferences: S. Coles, An Introduction to Statistical Modeling of Extreme Values, Springer, 2001, §4.3; J. R. M. Hosking and J. R. Wallis, \"Parameter and quantile estimation for the generalized Pareto distribution\", Technometrics 29(3), 1987.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.TailRules"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.ExtremeValueHalfwidth","page":"Scoring","text":"ExtremeValueHalfwidth(; tail rules...)\n\nA uniform outlier model whose half-width is the fitted tail's endpoint.\n\nThe uniform outlier density at a half-width estimated from each partition's complement: the endpoint u - ω/ξ of the generalized Pareto law the TailRules (its keywords) fit to the complement's upper tail.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.ExtremeValueHalfwidth"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.GeneralizedParetoOutliers","page":"Scoring","text":"GeneralizedParetoOutliers(; fit_per = :prefix, tail rules...)\n\nAn outlier model whose density is the fitted generalized Pareto law.\n\nThe fitted generalized Pareto law itself as the outlier density: the law of the TailRules fit, carried down to zero by threshold stability, with scale |ξ|·a at zero and endpoint a — the uniform density at ξ = -1, and the uniform one where there is no finite-endpoint fit. fit_per = :prefix refits the tail for every partition (exact, Θ(N²) per hypothesis); :hypothesis fits it once to the upper tail of all residuals (Θ(N), without the prefix-optimality guarantee).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.GeneralizedParetoOutliers"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.OutlierTerm","page":"Scoring","text":"OutlierTerm(cost)\n\nAn outlier model given by the caller's cost function.\n\nA caller's outlier term, for research baselines that are not one of the models above (block statistics of the complement): cost(k, z) is the complement's -log density for the partition of the k smallest residuals, z the sorted absolute residuals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.OutlierTerm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.tail_fit","page":"Scoring","text":"tail_fit(rules, z) -> (; outlier_halfwidth, fitted_endpoint, shape)\n\nThe tail fit of rules to the sorted values z.\n\nThe peaks-over-threshold fit of rules (a TailRules or an extreme-value outlier model) to the sorted values z: outlier_halfwidth is the uniform half-width (the fitted endpoint, raised to the largest value); fitted_endpoint is -Inf and shape NaN wherever the rules fall back (too few excesses, or no finite-endpoint fit). The sweep fits every prefix's complement the same way, in O(log n) each, from suffix sums it builds once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.tail_fit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.gpd_pwm_fit","page":"Scoring","text":"gpd_pwm_fit(n, sum_excess, sum_rank_excess) -> (shape, scale)\n\nThe generalized Pareto shape and scale of n excesses, from two sums.\n\nThe probability-weighted-moment fit of a generalized Pareto law G(y) = 1 - (1 + shape·y/scale)^(-1/shape) to n excesses, from their sum and their rank-weighted sum Σ_j j·y_(j) (ascending ranks), with the plotting positions (j - 0.35)/n (Hosking & Wallis 1987; Hosking's k is -shape). Taking the sums rather than the excesses lets the sweep fit every prefix's tail in O(1) from prefix sums.\n\n\n\n\n\ngpd_pwm_fit(excess) -> (shape, scale)\n\nThe same fit from the excesses themselves (any order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.gpd_pwm_fit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.gpd_support","page":"Scoring","text":"gpd_support(fit, z, lo, hi) -> endpoint\n\nThe support endpoint of the fitted generalized Pareto density.\n\nThe endpoint the generalized Pareto density scores with: the fitted endpoint where it lies beyond every value (for shape > -1 the density vanishes at the endpoint, so the largest value cannot sit on it), else the largest value plus the mean spacing of z[lo:hi] (positive unless every value ties) — equivariant under a change of units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.gpd_support"},{"category":"function","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.gpd_outlier_cost","page":"Scoring","text":"gpd_outlier_cost(z, endpoint, shape) -> Float64\n\nThe outlier cost of one residual under the generalized Pareto density.\n\n-log p(r) for one signed scalar residual of absolute value z under the generalized Pareto outlier density of endpoint endpoint and shape shape (GeneralizedPareto(0, σ, shape) with σ = |shape|·endpoint, halved for the sign): log 2 + log σ + (1 + 1/shape)·log(1 - z/endpoint); the uniform log(2·endpoint) where shape is not a finite-endpoint fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.gpd_outlier_cost"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.LossWidthScoring","page":"Scoring","text":"LossWidthScoring{DG}\n\nThe loss-width-parameterised scorings of both branches at codimension DG — the paper's \"threshold-based family\". A Union, not a supertype: \"takes a loss width\" cuts across the mixture branch and LossCostScoring, so it cannot be a supertype of both without implying a mixture underneath the losses.\n\nThis is the type the loss-width sweep entry points accept. Widening them to AbstractScoring would readmit StudentTUniformScoring, which has no loss width and would fail several frames down inside GPU staging instead of at the call site.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.LossWidthScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.MlesacScoring","page":"Scoring","text":"MlesacScoring{DG, T}\n\nMLESAC (GaU) at codimension DG: the Gaussian-uniform log-density at a fixed scale, normalised to 1 at d = 0 (see residual_quality). Tag \"mlesac\" (ALL_SCORING_TAGS). MlesacScoring(), MlesacScoring(dg), MlesacScoring(problem): the width-free grid family; MlesacScoring(dg; threshold = w): the scorer at loss width w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.MlesacScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.MagsacScoring","page":"Scoring","text":"MagsacScoring{DG, T}\n\nMAGSAC++ at codimension DG: the σ-marginalised quality, zero from the cutoff on; its loss width is that cutoff, _MAGSAC_SCALE_MULTIPLE·σ_max (see residual_quality). Tag \"magsac\"; the forms of MlesacScoring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.MagsacScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.WelschCostScoring","page":"Scoring","text":"WelschCostScoring{DG, T}\n\nThe Welsch cost as a loss-width score at codimension DG: 1 − ρ/w² with VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Welsch ρ, no mixture underneath. Tag \"welsch_cost\"; the forms of MlesacScoring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.WelschCostScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.SmoothTLSScoring","page":"Scoring","text":"SmoothTLSScoring{DG, T}\n\nThe smooth truncated-least-squares cost as a loss-width score at codimension DG: 1 − ρ/w² with VisualGeometryToolkitCore's smooth-TLS ρ. Tag \"smooth_tls\"; the forms of MlesacScoring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.SmoothTLSScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.GemanScoring","page":"Scoring","text":"GemanScoring{DG, T}\n\nThe Geman–McClure cost as a loss-width score at codimension DG: 1 − ρ/w² with VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Geman–McClure ρ. Tag \"geman\"; the forms of MlesacScoring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.GemanScoring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.loss_width_scoring","page":"Scoring","text":"loss_width_scoring(tag) -> LossWidthScoring\nloss_width_scoring(tag, dg::Integer) -> LossWidthScoring\nloss_width_scoring(tag, problem) -> LossWidthScoring\nloss_width_scoring(tag, ::Type{<:AbstractConstraintProblem}) -> LossWidthScoring\n\nThe width-free LossWidthScoring family a tag of ALL_SCORING_TAGS names, at a codimension: \"ransac\", \"msac\", \"mlesac\", \"magsac\", \"welsch_cost\", \"smooth_tls\" or \"geman\". A retired tag (\"gau\") is an ArgumentError naming its successor, and a tag of a family that takes no loss width (\"student_t_uniform\", \"sim_fit_pp\") is refused. The codimension is dg, or codimension(problem); bare, it is 1.\n\nSupply the codimension whenever the problem has one other than 1: the tag vocabulary must not be a back door that silently re-defaults DG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.loss_width_scoring"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/scoring/#RANSACScoringDoneRight.ALL_SCORING_TAGS","page":"Scoring","text":"ALL_SCORING_TAGS\n\nThe scoring families' TagRegistry: VisualGeometryToolkitCore's SCORING_TAGS (\"ransac\", \"msac\", \"student_t_uniform\", VisualGeometryToolkitCore's StudentTUniformScoring) merged with this package's — \"mlesac\", \"magsac\", \"welsch_cost\", \"smooth_tls\", \"geman\" (the loss-width families) and \"sim_fit_pp\" (SimFitPP, VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's SIMFIT++). The retired tags \"gau\", \"welsch\" and \"simfitpp\" name their successors. loss_width_scoring and every config loader read it; tag_of(ALL_SCORING_TAGS, x) is the writer side. It holds Core's SCORING_TAGS and is named apart from it: Core owns that name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RANSACScoringDoneRight.ALL_SCORING_TAGS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/experiments/#Experiments","page":"Experiments","text":"","title":"Experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/experiments/#Reproducing-the-paper","page":"Experiments","text":"The paper's figures are drawn by drivers in the research project neurips2026/, which has its own environment:\n\ncd neurips2026\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=. scripts/fetch_data.jl          # a machine without the data (neurips2026/data/README.md)\njulia --project=. scripts/fig_profile.jl --plan\njulia --project=. test/runtests.jl\nmake -C paper\n\nneurips2026/REPRODUCE.md maps every figure of the paper to its driver, its config of record and its data. The figures regenerate byte for byte from the six stored evaluation runs of record. The evaluation that produced those runs is documented there but not yet ported to the current stack.","title":"Reproducing the paper"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/experiments/#Scoring-studies-(retired)","page":"Experiments","text":"Two scoring studies and a decision probe used to ship beside the package as drivers in neurips2026/scripts/studies/. They were retired in 0.8.3 and are not reproducible from VisualGeometryToolkitEval 0.10 on: their pool of record (PT-E-SIFT-4K) is a pool format that VisualGeometryToolkitEval no longer reads. Their write-ups stay in neurips2026/notes/ as records of the runs they describe, and the drivers and configs are in the repository's history up to tag v0.8.2. Both studies exercised the selection rule alone, with no local optimization: every scorer scored the same stored minimal-sample hypotheses of the PhotoTourism essential-matrix pool, and every scorer was called from the package itself.\n\nThese are not the paper's Profile and Sensitivity experiments, whose figure drivers are neurips2026/scripts/fig_profile.jl and fig_sensitivity.jl (see the Overview and neurips2026/REPRODUCE.md).\n\nwrite-up question\nneurips2026/notes/sample_budget_miscal.md how selection improves with the hypothesis budget, calibrated and under a 32× miscalibrated scale\nneurips2026/notes/score_timing.md the per-hypothesis CPU cost of each scorer","title":"Scoring studies (retired)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/experiments/#Environment","page":"Experiments","text":"neurips2026/ is its own Julia project, not a dependency of the package: the drivers need VisualGeometryToolkitEval (pool IO, the run records), which the package deliberately does not depend on, and the concrete problem types of VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation, which the package itself uses only for SIMFIT++.\n\ncd neurips2026 && julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'","title":"Environment"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/experiments/#Sample-budget-under-scale-miscalibration-(the-write-up)","page":"Experiments","text":"Each scorer's operating point is read from the deployed cross-validation sidecar, tuned at calibration (ζ = 1) and not re-tuned; miscalibration multiplies every scorer's native width by the same ζ, which is principled for both families because both natives are linear in the noise scale. The budget is counted in minimal samples, from 100 to 4 000, on 8 800 pairs of four held-out test scenes.\n\nThe write-up reports mAA@10° and median pose error at every budget for the Jeffreys score, MSAC, GaU, RANSAC and MAGSAC++. Calibrated, the five are within 0.013 mAA of one another at 4 000 samples; at ζ = 32 the Jeffreys score retains 83 % of its calibrated mAA and keeps improving with the budget, while the four baselines retain 16–23 % and get worse as the budget grows.","title":"Sample budget under scale miscalibration (the write-up)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/experiments/#Per-hypothesis-scoring-cost-(the-write-up)","page":"Experiments","text":"Single-threaded, on the stored residuals of one PhotoTourism scene, every scorer at one shared operating point; the figure is the minimum over three repetitions, per hypothesis scored.\n\nThe sort-and-sweep costs more than RANSAC and MSAC and less than GaU and closed-form MAGSAC++; its cost grows linearly with the number of points, since both its radix sort and its sweep are linear. The write-up's MAGSAC++ lookup-table row was measured with a tabulated scorer that has since been removed from the package; the current driver times the exact forms only.","title":"Per-hypothesis scoring cost (the write-up)"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"<div id=\"landing\" class=\"landing\">\n<header class=\"landing-hero\">\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__text\">\n    <p class=\"landing-name\" style=\"--landing-name-chars: 25\">RANSAC Scoring Done Right</p>\n    <h1 class=\"landing-title\">Marginalize the Scale, Then Choose the Inliers</h1>\n    <p class=\"landing-tagline\">James Pritts · Felix Seegräber · Kevin Köser\nMarine Data Science\nKiel University\nNeurIPS 2026</p>\n    <div class=\"landing-actions\">\n<a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--brand\" href=\"#Getting-started\">Getting started</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"api/scoring/\">API Reference</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.27385\">Paper</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/RANSACScoringDoneRight.jl\">Code</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__image\">\n    <div class=\"landing-hero__image-container\">\n      <img class=\"docs-light-only\" src=\"assets/landing/hero_scoring_light.png\" alt=\"A line model with noisy points; points the score selects as inliers are green, outliers red, with inliers drawn larger. Below, the residuals sorted ascending with the marginal score of every candidate partition, peaking at the selected inlier count.\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n      <img class=\"docs-dark-only\" src=\"assets/landing/hero_scoring_dark.png\" alt=\"A line model with noisy points; points the score selects as inliers are green, outliers red, with inliers drawn larger. Below, the residuals sorted ascending with the marginal score of every candidate partition, peaking at the selected inlier count.\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</header>\n<section class=\"landing-features\" aria-label=\"Features\">\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"api/scoring/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🎯</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Score a hypothesis</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>StudentTUniformScoring</code> with <code>score!</code> (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's scoring contract) sorts a model's residuals and sweeps every partition: the score and the selected inlier count, with no threshold and no inlier scale.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"api/pools/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🗂️</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Select from a hypothesis pool</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>best_pool_model</code> picks the best stored model of a benchmark pair under the marginal score or any loss-width baseline (RANSAC, MSAC, GaU, MAGSAC++), on the pair's residual matrix.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"api/pools/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">⚡</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Evaluate a width grid on CPU or GPU</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>stage_ig_shared_from_pairs</code> stages a pool once; <code>grid_evaluate</code> scores it at every width of a grid with KernelAbstractions kernels on <code>CPU()</code> or CUDA.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"api/posterior/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">⚖️</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Weight points for refinement</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>consensus_s2</code> and <code>PosteriorWeightState</code> (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's toolkit) turn the selected partition into a scale estimate and Student-t posterior weights for local optimization.</p>\n  </a>\n</section>\n</div>\n\nA closed-form RANSAC score in which the inlier scale is genuinely absent. The scale is marginalized under a conjugate inverse-gamma prior for each inlier partition, and the partition is optimized by sort-and-sweep in O(N log N).","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Scoring-under-scale-miscalibration","page":"Home","text":"<table class=\"sel-miscal\">\n<thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:center\">Median pose error</th><th style=\"text-align:center\">mAA@10°</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:center\"><img class=\"docs-light-only\" src=\"assets/landing/sel_miscal_median_light.png\" alt=\"Median pose error of each score against the scale corruption ζ, from 1/128 to 128, on PhotoTourism essential matrices without local optimization. All scores tie near 2.3° at ζ = 1; away from it Stu-tU stays lowest over most of the range, while the threshold-based scores leave the 6° axis under inflation.\"/><img class=\"docs-dark-only\" src=\"assets/landing/sel_miscal_median_dark.png\" alt=\"Median pose error of each score against the scale corruption ζ, from 1/128 to 128, on PhotoTourism essential matrices without local optimization. All scores tie near 2.3° at ζ = 1; away from it Stu-tU stays lowest over most of the range, while the threshold-based scores leave the 6° axis under inflation.\"/></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:center\"><img class=\"docs-light-only\" src=\"assets/landing/sel_miscal_maa_light.png\" alt=\"mAA@10° of each score against the scale corruption ζ at the same operating points. Stu-tU keeps about 0.45 at 128×, where the threshold-based scores fall below 0.15.\"/><img class=\"docs-dark-only\" src=\"assets/landing/sel_miscal_maa_dark.png\" alt=\"mAA@10° of each score against the scale corruption ζ at the same operating points. Stu-tU keeps about 0.45 at 128×, where the threshold-based scores fall below 0.15.\"/></td>\n</tr></tbody>\n</table>\n\nScoring under scale miscalibration, without local optimization (PhotoTourism essential matrices; 11 test and 10 validation scenes). Each score's hyperparameter is tuned at the correct scale and then evaluated with the scale it is given multiplied by ζ, from 128 times too small to 128 times too large, without retuning. Left: the scene-averaged median pose error, with the axis clipped at 6° so the near-tied calibrated regime stays readable (the threshold-based scores leave the panel under inflation and reach 33°–47° at 128×). Right: mAA@10° at the same operating points, which are tuned on the median. Stu-tU is this package's scale-free marginal score. The baselines are the Welsch and Geman–McClure scores, RANSAC, MSAC, MAGSAC++, the Gaussian–uniform score (GaU) and SIMFIT++, which re-estimates the inlier threshold per pair from held-out residuals of the hypothesis selected on a random half of the correspondences. Shaded bands are scene-clustered BCa bootstrap confidence intervals. From the supplement of Robust Local Optimization Done Right (ACCV 2026).","title":"Scoring under scale miscalibration"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-method","page":"Home","text":"The score marginalizes the inlier scale analytically and chooses the inlier set with one sort-and-sweep over the residuals (paper, Algorithm 1).\n\n<pre class=\"pseudocode\">\n\\begin{algorithm}\n\\caption{Sort-and-sweep computation of the scale-free marginal score. The algorithm returns the best-scoring prefix $\\mathcal{I}^* = \\{(1),\\ldots,(k^*)\\}$ in $O(N\\log N)$ time.}\n\\begin{algorithmic}\n\\REQUIRE per-point scores $q_1,\\ldots,q_N$; codimension $d_g$; model parameters $n_\\theta$; outlier half-width $a$; prior parameters $(\\alpha_0, \\beta_0)$\n\\ENSURE best score $S^*$; optimal inlier count $k^*$\n\\STATE Sort so that $q_{(1)} \\leq q_{(2)} \\leq \\cdots \\leq q_{(N)}$\n\\STATE $k_{\\min} \\gets \\min\\bigl(\\lfloor n_\\theta/d_g \\rfloor,\\, N\\bigr)$ \\COMMENT{largest prefix with $\\nu \\leq 0$}\n\\STATE $\\mathrm{RSS} \\gets \\sum_{j=1}^{k_{\\min}} q_{(j)}$ \\COMMENT{seed the zero-DOF prefix}\n\\STATE $S^* \\gets -\\infty$, $\\quad k^* \\gets 0$\n\\FOR{$k = k_{\\min}+1, \\ldots, N$}\n\\STATE $\\mathrm{RSS} \\gets \\mathrm{RSS} + q_{(k)}$\n\\STATE $\\nu \\gets k\\,d_g - n_\\theta$ \\COMMENT{positive DOF guaranteed}\n\\STATE $S \\gets \\log\\Gamma\\bigl(\\nu/2 + \\alpha_0\\bigr) - \\bigl(\\tfrac{\\nu}{2}+\\alpha_0\\bigr)\\log\\bigl(\\tfrac{\\mathrm{RSS}}{2}+\\beta_0\\bigr) - \\tfrac{\\nu}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - (N-k)\\,d_g\\,\\log(2a)$\n\\IF{$S &gt; S^*$}\n\\STATE $S^* \\gets S$, $\\quad k^* \\gets k$\n\\ENDIF\n\\ENDFOR\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n</pre>\n\nThe Student-t has no threshold, only the outlier half-width: the sweep selects each hypothesis's inlier partition, and its boundary follows that hypothesis's own scale, hat s = sqrtmathrmRSS_k^*  nu.","title":"The method"},{"category":"section","location":"#Abstract","page":"Home","text":"The most widely used RANSAC variants score candidate models by counting inliers or summing per-point scores that saturate beyond a residual threshold. Every such score requires a user-supplied parameter that is a function of the inlier scale, which must itself be estimated from contaminated data. We remove this dependence by reversing the usual order of inference: rather than estimating the scale and then scoring against it, we marginalize σ analytically in closed form under a conjugate Inverse-Gamma prior for a fixed inlier partition, then optimize over partitions. A single closed-form expression spans the non-informative Jeffreys limit and informative empirical-Bayes priors, so the same score adapts across data-rich and data-scarce regimes without any change to the algorithm. The proposed RANSAC score is the first in which the inlier scale is genuinely absent from the formula. The score admits O(N log N) computation via sort-and-sweep. On a benchmark of nearly 70 000 image pairs spanning different two-view estimation problems and both engineered and learned feature pipelines, the proposed score exceeds the state of the art (RANSAC, MSAC, GaU, MAGSAC++): it stays nearly flat under threshold miscalibration where baselines degrade, reaches near-optimal accuracy from as few as two validation pairs where baselines need ~100× more, and tightens its prior regularization as validation data grows scarce.","title":"Abstract"},{"category":"section","location":"#Install","page":"Home","text":"Pkg — RANSACScoringDoneRight is registered in the General registry (Julia 1.13+). The example below also loads VisualGeometryToolkitCore, which defines the scoring contract:\n\npkg> add RANSACScoringDoneRight VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\ngit — clone the repository and instantiate it (every dependency, the neurips2026/ project's VisualGeometryToolkitEval included, is in General):\n\ngit clone https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/RANSACScoringDoneRight.jl.git && cd RANSACScoringDoneRight.jl && julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-started","page":"Home","text":"Score one hypothesis from its residuals: the sweep sorts them, scores every candidate inlier partition with the scale marginalized out, and returns the best score with its inlier count, whose scale estimate follows in closed form.\n\nusing RANSACScoringDoneRight\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore: StudentTUniformScoring, JeffreysPrior, FlatLabelPrior,\n    scoring_workspace, score!, inlier_count, consensus_order, consensus_s2\nusing Random\n\n# The residuals of one line hypothesis (codimension 1): 80 inliers with noise\n# scale 0.01 and 20 outliers spread uniformly over [-0.5, 0.5].\nrng = Xoshiro(7)\nn = 100\nr = [0.01 .* randn(rng, 80); rand(rng, 20) .- 0.5]\n\n# a line has 2 parameters; outlier half-width 0.5; the scale and label priors (Jeffreys, flat) are required\nscoring = StudentTUniformScoring(2, 0.5; scale_prior = JeffreysPrior(), label_prior = FlatLabelPrior())\nW = scoring_workspace(scoring, n, 1)      # sweep buffers, reusable across hypotheses\nq = Vector{Float64}(undef, n)\nS = score!(W, scoring, q, r, n)           # the score, with the scale integrated out\nk = inlier_count(scoring, W)              # the selected inlier count: about 80\ns2 = consensus_s2(scoring, r, consensus_order(scoring, W), k; model_dof = 2, d_g = 1)\nsqrt(s2)                                  # the partition's scale ŝ: about 0.01\n\nStudentTUniformScoring(2, 0.5; scale_prior = IGPrior(α₀, β₀), label_prior = FlatLabelPrior()) scores under an informative prior instead (its workspace is built from the scoring, so it carries that prior).","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Start here: the Overview — scoring versus selection, the scorers, the scale priors, native widths and the pool frame.\nHow to: Experiments reproduces the paper's figures, with the write-ups of the retired scoring studies.\nExplanation: Conventions — units, frames and widths; Working on the stack — the contracts the package implements, its GPU evaluators and its vocabulary.\nReference: Scoring, Posterior weighting, Pools, grids and tuning and Internals.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Method","page":"Home","text":"Reverse the order of inference. Threshold-based scores eliminate the inlier/outlier labels first and are left with a hyperparameter that is a quantile of the inlier scale. Here the scale is marginalized first, analytically, under a conjugate inverse-gamma prior for a fixed inlier partition; the partition is then optimized.\nOne closed form, two regimes. The non-informative Jeffreys prior is the limit of the inverse-gamma family, so the same expression covers the Jeffreys score, which needs no validation data, and empirical-Bayes priors fit from a small validation set (IG-MLE by type-II maximum likelihood; IG-Robust with α₀ = 1 and a median-anchored β₀).\nSort and sweep. The score depends on the residual sum of squares, the inlier count and the outlier half-width a, so every partition of the sorted residuals is scored in a single sweep: O(N log N) per hypothesis.\nThe outlier half-width is not a scale in disguise. a parameterizes the uniform outlier component and is tied to the inlier scale by no quantile relation; where the data dominate the prior, the model the score selects is largely insensitive to it.\nIsolate the score. Following Shekhovtsov (2025), every method scores the same per-pair pool of minimal-sample hypotheses and differs only in its scoring function and its single hyperparameter.","title":"Method"},{"category":"section","location":"#Results","page":"Home","text":"Robust to miscalibration in the data-rich regime. On PhotoTourism and ETH3D the median error of the proposed scores is nearly flat across a two-decade window of the hyperparameter (varying by less than 0.02° on PhotoTourism), while the threshold-based baselines degrade off their optimum.\nSame ceiling at the optimum. Tuned optimally, the proposed scores and the threshold-based baselines reach the same accuracy; the difference is in how much tuning that takes.\nNear-optimal from two validation pairs. The proposed scores reach near-optimal expected test error at |V| = 2, where the baselines need ~100× more validation data; on PhotoTourism and ETH3D their trial-to-trial spread at |V| = 2 is 30–100× smaller.\nThe prior matters when data are scarce. On HEB, with about ten inliers per pair, the median-anchored IG-Robust prior regularizes the per-partition scale and gives the lowest error at every validation budget, while Jeffreys, which gives that regularization up, is the least stable.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-projects","page":"Home","text":"Part of the Visual Geometry Toolkit, Julia packages for robust visual geometry from Kiel University:\n\nBlobBoards: robust markers for accurate pose (BMVC 2026)\nRobust Local Optimization Done Right: score sharp, refine soft (ACCV 2026)","title":"Related projects"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"The paper is on arXiv (PDF). If you use this project for your research, please cite:\n\n@inproceedings{Pritts2026RansacScoring,\n  title         = {{RANSAC} Scoring Done Right},\n  author        = {Pritts, James and Seegr{\\\"a}ber, Felix and K{\\\"o}ser, Kevin},\n  booktitle     = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  year          = {2026},\n  eprint        = {2606.27385},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  primaryClass  = {cs.LG}\n}","title":"Citation"}]
}
